WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.985 --> 00:00:12.630
Here at Team Wow, customer service is more than just taking orders.

2
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.035
It's the experience we create with every interaction.

3
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:20.491
Every customer should feel welcomed, heard, and valued from the moment we answer

4
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.951
the phone to the moment we hand them their order.

5
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:28.320
Our tone should always be friendly, professional, and informed, and we listen with

6
00:00:28.381 --> 00:00:33.450
intention, communicate clearly, and stay positive even during difficult situations.

7
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.661
Remember, customers may not always remember exactly what was said, but they will

8
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.790
always remember how we made them feel.

9
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.840
When handling customer concerns, we follow LEADS.

10
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:47.640
Listen,

11
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.370
empathize,

12
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:52.890
apologize,

13
00:00:54.511 --> 00:00:54.750
do,

14
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:56.761
and stand.

15
00:00:58.051 --> 00:01:01.620
At Team Wow, we don't take complaints personally.

16
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.770
We use them as opportunities to create loyal customers.

17
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:11.415
Great service builds trust, trust builds relationships, and relationships, well,

18
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:17.400
they build our business. All right, team, let's take a moment and

19
00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:22.380
really dive into LEADS. I want you to think of customer concerns like penalties on

20
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:28.321
the play. Here's the truth. Competing at a top-tier level requires a fanatical work

21
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.836
ethic. We move fast, we operate in controlled chaos, we juggle multiple assignments

22
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.060
at once. We're answering phones, making products, helping customers, checking

23
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.051
orders, supporting teammates, and trying to beat the clock all at once.

24
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.961
You see how that can be a little overwhelming?

25
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.525
And just like in sports, even the best athletes are going to make mistakes.

26
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.105
From time to time, somebody's going to miss an assignment, commit an infraction, or

27
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.726
make the wrong play. But mistakes are not what separate average athletes from

28
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:03.780
superstars. The defining factor is what happens next.

29
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.141
Do we lose our composure? Do we point fingers?

30
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:14.010
Do we make excuses? Or do we reset, take ownership, and make the next play?

31
00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:20.580
We believe every customer concern gives us another opportunity, an opportunity to

32
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:26.280
recover trust, solve the problem, and turn a disappointed customer into a loyal

33
00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.200
customer. So everybody, get out your playbooks because we're about to draw this one

34
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:31.440
up.

35
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.830
This formation is called LEADS, and it's a play we'll run every single game day, so

36
00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:42.630
make sure you take notes. Listen closely because when a customer concern comes your

37
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.610
way, this is how we respond.

38
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.340
First up is listen.

39
00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:53.760
That's what L stands for. Before we try to solve a problem, we have to understand

40
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.351
the problem. That means giving the customer our full attention, allowing them to

41
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.320
finish, and asking questions when something is unclear.

42
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.490
Do not interrupt, and do not assume.

43
00:03:06.345 --> 00:03:09.660
And do not begin planning your defense while the customer is still talking.

44
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.129
Great athletes watch the entire play before reacting.

45
00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.820
Great customer service works the same way.

46
00:03:17.835 --> 00:03:20.476
A strong listening response might sound like this:

47
00:03:22.515 --> 00:03:27.031
"Let me make sure I understand. You ordered a large pepperoni pizza, but the pizza

48
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.460
you received has sausage. Is that correct?"

49
00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:34.770
Repeating the concern back shows the customer that you heard them and gives them a

50
00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:38.250
chance to clarify anything that may have been misunderstood.

51
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.230
Remember, if we misunderstand the problem, we may solve the wrong problem.

52
00:03:43.951 --> 00:03:48.570
So a coaching point for that is listen to understand, not just to respond.

53
00:03:50.281 --> 00:03:55.800
Next is empathize, E. Empathy means recognizing how the

54
00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:00.781
situation affected the customer. You do not have to agree with every word they say.

55
00:04:00.796 --> 00:04:04.815
You do not have to accept disrespectful behavior, but you should acknowledge that

56
00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:09.541
their frustration, their disappointment, or inconvenience is real.

57
00:04:09.555 --> 00:04:12.404
A strong empathy statement might sound like this:

58
00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:18.091
"I understand how frustrating that would be, especially after waiting for your

59
00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:18.390
order."

60
00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:24.630
That one sentence can completely change the tone of the conversation because the

61
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.080
customer now feels heard instead of challenged.

62
00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:32.085
And team, be careful not to skip empathy and jump straight into explaining what

63
00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.451
happened. Customers usually do not want our internal play-by-play.

64
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.275
They want to know that we understand how the mistake affected them.

65
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.925
Good coach's point to write down for here is connect with the customer before

66
00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.035
correcting the problem.

67
00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:49.650
The third step is apologize.

68
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.975
A sincere apology is not a weakness, guys. It's ownership.

69
00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.330
Even when you personally did not make the mistake, you still represent the team.

70
00:05:00.841 --> 00:05:06.630
We do not say, "That wasn't me." We do not say, "The driver

71
00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:12.120
forgot it." And we definitely do not say, "You must have ordered it wrong." Those

72
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.890
responses may explain the situation, but they do not recover the customer.

73
00:05:16.951 --> 00:05:22.755
Instead, say, "Hey, I'm sorry we didn't get that right." Keep it clear, sincere,

74
00:05:23.385 --> 00:05:28.591
and free of excuses. An apology tells the customer that we recognize the standard

75
00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.140
was missed and that we care enough to address it.

76
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:37.230
A key coaching point is own the experience even when you didn't personally cause

77
00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:37.920
the problem.

78
00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.260
Next comes do what it takes, D.

79
00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:47.281
This is where we move from words to action.

80
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:53.475
Listening is important, empathy is important, apologizing is very important, but

81
00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.130
eventually, we have to solve the problem.

82
00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.244
A strong response might sound like this: "Hey, I'm going to have the correct pizza

83
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:07.080
remade immediately." The solution should be clear, realistic, and within

84
00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.860
company guidelines. Do not promise something you cannot deliver.

85
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.805
Do not make the customer guess what happens next, and make sure to tell them

86
00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.695
exactly what action you are taking.

87
00:06:17.281 --> 00:06:20.520
This is where you show the difference between somebody who talks about customer

88
00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.419
service and somebody who actually delivers it.

89
00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.130
Your coaching point is solutions win customers back, not excuses.

90
00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.930
The final step is stand by your promise.

91
00:06:34.591 --> 00:06:37.410
This is where a lot of customer recoveries fall apart.

92
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.355
We tell the customers we're going to fix the issue, but then no one follows

93
00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:46.500
through. Standing by your promise means giving the customer a clear expectation and

94
00:06:46.561 --> 00:06:50.790
making sure it actually happens. A strong response might sound like this:

95
00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:56.010
"Apologies once again. The remake should be ready in about 10 minutes.

96
00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.785
I'll personally check it before I bring it to you." Now the customer knows,

97
00:07:02.401 --> 00:07:06.480
one, what's happening, two, how long it should take, and three, who is taking

98
00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:11.145
ownership. And then we must follow through because a broken promise after a mistake

99
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.445
can really damage trust even more than the original problem.

100
00:07:15.091 --> 00:07:19.500
Your coaching point would be, do what you said you would do and confirm that the

101
00:07:19.561 --> 00:07:25.335
customer is taken care of. A strong LEADS response will sound something like

102
00:07:25.335 --> 00:07:29.280
this when it all comes together. "Let me make sure I understand.

103
00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.540
You received one pizza, but the second pizza from your order was not delivered.

104
00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.399
I understand how frustrating that is, especially when you were expecting it a while

105
00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.320
ago. I'm sorry we missed that. I'm going to contact the store and have the missing

106
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.760
pizza prepared immediately. I'll give you an updated delivery time, and I'll follow

107
00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:52.500
up to make sure it reaches you." We made sure we listened.

108
00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:53.790
We confirmed what was missing.

109
00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.710
We had empathy and acknowledged the inconvenience.

110
00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:04.065
We apologized and took ownership, and we did do, gave a clear

111
00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:07.050
solution. And lastly, we stood.

112
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:12.436
We set expectations and promised follow-through by our stand.

113
00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:17.610
Team, LEADS is not about memorizing five letters for a quiz.

114
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.826
It is about developing the response that becomes automatic when something goes

115
00:08:21.841 --> 00:08:26.581
wrong. Remember, listen before you react, empathize before you explain,

116
00:08:27.091 --> 00:08:32.670
apologize without excuses, do what it takes, and lastly, stand by your

117
00:08:32.701 --> 00:08:37.170
promise. Mistakes may create the penalty, but the next play gives us the

118
00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:41.311
opportunity to recover. Average teams complain about the call.

119
00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.080
Great teams make the next play. Now let's see if you're ready to run LEADS.
